WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.980
Welcome to fascinating frontiers, Episode 65, coming to you on May 8th, 2026.

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:08.720
Here's your space and science briefing.

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:16.140
NASA and SpaceX are preparing a new cargo launch to restock the International Space Station.

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:26.260
NASA and SpaceX are preparing a Dragon cargo mission that will carry food, experiments, and spare parts to the orbiting laboratory.

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:32.580
The flight will keep continuous science operations running aboard the International Space Station.

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:36.440
It will also sustain crew activities without interruption.

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:44.080
This kind of resupply is a routine yet essential link in the ongoing logistics chain that supports the station.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:51.140
Engineers focus on reliable technology and precise orbital operations to make each mission succeed.

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:59.320
Now we turn from steady station support to the longer isolation tests that future Mars crews will face.

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:07.600
A NASA crew has now reached the 200-day mark inside a closed habitat that simulates conditions on the red planet.

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:13.920
The team manages water, oxygen, and waste without any resupply from outside.

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:20.380
Psychologists track mood and performance while engineers collect data on daily operations.

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:27.220
The results with shape habitat design and crew routines for actual Mars missions in the years ahead.

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:33.560
Small comforts and personal schedules prove surprisingly important during months of isolation.

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:40.480
This test shows how humans adapt when every resource must be recycled on the spot.

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:47.760
From Earthbound Mars practice, we move next to companies already building hardware for the lunar surface.

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:54.060
Lunar Outposts has secured $30 million in new funding for its Artemis Rovers.

00:01:54.520 --> 00:02:00.780
The company is redesigning the vehicles to match updated NASA landing plans for the Artemis program.

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:07.440
The capital will support hardware revisions and surface testing ahead of planned lunar operations.

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:14.780
This investment keeps private sector technology aligned with the government timeline for returning to the moon.

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:21.160
Business decisions like this one directly influence how quickly Rovers can be ready for flight.

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:29.340
Shifting from lunar surface plans, new eyes and orbit are already mapping energy and industry on Earth.

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:37.900
The Hotsat 2 satellite has returned high-resolution infrared views of oil facilities in Cuba, India and Australia.

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:43.320
These thermal images help monitor industrial heat output in detail.

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:48.780
The data also support commercial energy tracking across multiple countries.

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:56.120
The mission adds practical Earth observation capability that complements larger astronomy projects.

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:03.200
Engineers design the satellite to capture heat signatures that visible light sensors often miss.

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:10.960
From watching energy use on our own planet, we look outward to counting worlds around other stars.

00:03:11.720 --> 00:03:18.420
A NASA telescope has added over 10,000 new exoplanets to the catalog of known worlds.

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:23.380
These discoveries expand our list of planets orbiting distant stars.

00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:30.760
Continued observations will refine statistics on planet sizes and orbital distances across the galaxy.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:36.340
The growth represents a quiet but steady expansion of our galactic senses.

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:43.000
Each new world helps astronomers understand how common different planetary types truly are.

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:49.500
From distant planets we turn now to the hidden structures inside galaxies we can already resolve.

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:55.960
You know what is fascinating about how infrared eyes reveal hidden galactic bars?

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:08.280
If you could shrink the Milky Way to the size of a dinner plate, the central bar would still stretch across most of that plate, like a cosmic axle driving the galaxy rotation.

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:13.060
Infrared light slips past the dust that blocks visible starlight.

00:04:13.060 --> 00:04:18.660
This lets telescopes map the dense lanes of older stars that form these bars.

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:29.040
In Galaxy M77, the near infrared view shows the bar feeding gas into a tight ring where new stars ignite at furious rates.

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:38.900
The same wavelengths expose how the bar gravitational pole funnels material inward on time scales of just a few hundred million years.

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:50.680
We can watch the process in thousands of galaxies, yet no one has fully explained why some spirals develop strong bars while others remain smooth and featureless disks.

00:04:51.580 --> 00:04:59.840
The hidden trigger that decides whether a galaxy grows a bar or stays bar free remains one of the open questions in galactic evolution.

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:13.080
Before we go, keep an eye on the next set of solar surface images expected from ground-based telescopes as activity in Sunspot regions continues to evolve.

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:16.840
That covers today's space and science news.

00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:21.320
Share this with a fellow space enthusiast if you found it interesting.

00:05:22.100 --> 00:05:24.440
I am Patrick in Vancouver, see you tomorrow.

00:05:24.440 --> 00:05:32.940
